  "question": {
    "text": "If a drop of liquid breaks into smaller droplets, it results in lowering of temperature of the droplets. Let a drop of radius <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi></math>, break into <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>N</mi></math> small droplets each of radius <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>r</mi></math>, then decrease (drop) in temperature <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Q</mi></math> (given, specific heat of liquid drop<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mi>S</mi></math> and surface tension <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mi>T</mi></math> )",
